well we're here in the city of odessa in  southern ukraine and this city is widely  thought to be one of vladimir putin's  next targets right on the black sea as a  result people here have been preparing  in a number of ways  and this is one of them you see this  staircase that has just been opened up  here leads down to a series of tunnels  the catacombs below the city actually go  down about four stories very deep and  they were dug back in the 19th century  at the time people  were using the materials from below the  ground to help build houses above during  the second world war these tunnels were  turned into shelters and they are being  repurposed once again with the looming  threat of invasion from russia and now  anytime people in this area hear a siren  warning of a possible russian air strike  they come down into these tunnels  hundreds of them at a time potentially  and as you can see local residents here  have been busy at work they have set up  electricity here  there's even wi-fi  wireless internet setup in these tunnels  other people have brought down food  supplies  water bedding allowing them if needed to  stay down here for a couple of days now  before russia's recent invasion these  catacombs had been effectively closed  people could take a tour of them if they  made a request to the city beforehand  and had a tour guide guide them through  but now they have become reopened  volunteers helping to bring them back to  life and these 200 year old tunnels are  now providing a lifeline for a city  under threat  jeff semple global news odessa ukraine
 

